Based on the plan and diff samples, I have enough context to select targeted archetypes. The core change is a complex shell refactor: adding `--with-plan-size` mode to `design-postplan-emit.sh`, collapsing multiple fat fences into thin `call → echo → case rc` forms, strict sentinel-write contracts per site and per return path, strict KV/FD3 output discipline with suppressed helper output, and a separate release step 7 root-resolution rewrite.

{"archetypes":[{"name":"sentinel-write-contracts","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan defines very precise per-site sentinel write rules (initial rc0 writes both step-2b and step-2b.5; rc12/13 Split entry writes step-2b; Refine/Continue returns write both; Gate-B Override writes step-2b.5 before Step 3.6; etc.) that are invisible to generic correctness checks but could silently break pause/resume if any arm is missing.","prompt_body":"Focus exclusively on sentinel write completeness across all merged thin-fence sites in skills/design/SKILL.md, skills/design/references/approval-gates.md, and skills/design/references/discussion-rounds.md. For each merged call site (initial Step 2b, Gate B shared post-apply, discussion-round2, Step 1e Gate A re-entry), verify that every rc arm writes exactly the sentinels mandated by the plan: rc0 at initial site writes both .completed/step-2b and .completed/step-2b.5; rc12/rc13 Split entry writes .completed/step-2b; Refine return writes .completed/step-2b and .completed/step-2b.5; no-split Continue writes .completed/step-2b and .completed/step-2b.5; Gate-B rc12 Override writes/updates .completed/step-2b.5 before Step 3.6. Check retained Step 3 LOOP_STATUS=plan-size-trigger paths in plan-review-loop.sh for the same non-exiting return sentinel contracts. Flag any arm where a sentinel write is absent, written in the wrong order, or written after a branch that should not reach it. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"output-kv-discipline","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan prohibits stdout KV emission in --with-plan-size mode (KVs go to result-env only), requires FD3 for all display output, and requires that rc2/rc3 append-helper stdout/stderr (APPENDED=, LOG=) never leak into merged display or machine-readable output; violations silently corrupt downstream KV parsers.","prompt_body":"Audit the output discipline in skills/design/scripts/design-postplan-emit.sh for the --with-plan-size code path. Verify: no KEY=VALUE lines are emitted to stdout in merged mode (all KVs go to .design-postplan-emit-result.env only); user-facing status, WARN bodies, and breadcrumbs go through emit on FD 3 not stdout; the rc2/rc3 nonfatal plan-size path redirects all append-tool-failure.sh stdout and stderr so APPENDED= and LOG= never appear on merged display output; the nested check-plan-size.sh subprocess is invoked with LARCH_QUIET_DISABLE=1 and only its stdout is parsed for KVs while stderr is captured to a sidecar. Also check plan-review-loop.sh: on rc2/rc3, verify that append-tool-failure.sh output is redirected to /dev/null (or equivalent) with || true so helper KVs do not pollute loop stdout. Flag any place where a KV line could reach stdout in merged mode or where helper output could leak. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"bash32-portability","focus_area":"code-quality","weight":1,"rationale":"BASH_AUTHORING.md mandates Bash 3.2 compatibility for all committed shell scripts; new code in design-postplan-emit.sh, plan-review-loop.sh, and harness scripts must avoid declare -A, nameref, mapfile, ${var^^}, &>> and similar Bash 4+ constructs that silently fail or misbehave on macOS system Bash.","prompt_body":"Scan every shell file touched by this diff — primarily skills/design/scripts/design-postplan-emit.sh, skills/design/scripts/plan-review-loop.sh, scripts/test-design-structure.sh, and any new harness scripts — for Bash 4+ constructs forbidden by the project's BASH_AUTHORING.md: associative arrays (declare -A / typeset -A), namerefs (declare -n / local -n), mapfile / readarray, parameter case conversion (${var^^} / ${var^} / ${var,,} / ${var,}), append-all redirection (&>>), and coprocs. Also check for the renderer substitution safety issue: ${var//pattern/$replacement} when $replacement may contain user or file content with & characters. Report each occurrence with its file and line range and suggest the Bash 3.2-compatible alternative. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"release-step7-root-resolution","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The release step 7 rewrite adds a multi-priority root resolution algorithm (active CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT vs installed metadata vs CURRENT_VERSION fallback vs sole-version-dir fallback) with state written to release-step7.env and read back in Step 8; priority inversions or state-persistence failures could silently apply the wrong sparse allowlist or suppress the restart message.","prompt_body":"Review the release step 7 and step 8 prose changes in .claude/skills/release/SKILL.md and any new scripts (release-step7-root.sh, release-step7-root.md). Verify the root resolution priority order is correctly implemented: active CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT (cache-shaped and exists) wins over installed-metadata-derived path, which wins over CURRENT_VERSION prepare fallback, which wins over the sole-version-dir last fallback. Check that the sole-version-dir fallback correctly fails open (returns no root) when zero or multiple version dirs exist. Verify that release-step7.env is written atomically (via tmp+mv), that Step 8 reads it with awk rather than source, and that all three boolean flags default to false when the file is absent or PR_LIST_FILE is unavailable. Check that upgrade_out KV parsing uses string matching (not source) and that CONE_RECONCILED=true triggers RESTART_REQUIRED=true. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
